1
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,380
Reading Task

2
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:07,100
Finding the Solution

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:09,440
Do you like puzzles?

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:11,080
Euler did.

5
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:14,900
Did you solve the one you heard for the listening task?

6
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:19,240
Well, don't worry, Euler didn't either!

7
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:22,400
As he loved mathematical puzzles,

8
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:25,560
he wanted to know why this one wouldn't work.

9
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:47,440
He just had to know why.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,900
So he decided to look at the problem another way.

11
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:56,860
He drew himself a picture of the town and the seven bridges like the one above.

12
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,760
He marked the land and the bridges.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,640
He noticed that some points had three lines going to them (A,

14
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:24,460
B and C) and one had five (D).

15
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:29,180
He wondered if this was important and why the puzzle would not work.

16
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:34,840
As three and five are odd numbers he called them "odd" points.

17
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,440
He counted the lines going to points A,

18
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,380
B, C and D.

19
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:03,460
This time they were different.

20
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:09,840
Two of them had even numbers of lines (B had two and D had four).

21
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:16,360
Two and four are both even numbers so Euler called them "even" points.

22
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:33,800
Using this new diagram Euler started at point A,

23
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,560
went along the straight line to B and then to C.

24
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:42,740
Then he followed the curved line through D and back to A.

25
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:49,500
Finally he followed the other curved line from A back through D to C where

26
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:50,580
he finished the pattern.

27
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:52,660
This time it worked.

28
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:57,400
He had been able to go over the figure visiting each point

29
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:02,640
but not going over any line twice or lifting his pencil from the page.

30
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:05,880
Euler became very excited.

31
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,940
Now he knew that the number of odd points was the key to the puzzle.

32
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:16,660
However, you still needed some even points in your figure if you wanted it to work.

33
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:19,420
So Euler looked for a general rule:

34
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:23,180
If a figure has more than two odd points,

35
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:29,520
you cannot go over it without lifting your pencil from the page or going over a line twice.

36
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:34,760
Quickly he went to his textbooks to find some more figures.

37
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:39,200
He looked at the four diagrams shown below and found that

38
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:43,460
when he used his rule, he could tell if he could go over the whole figure

39
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:45,800
without taking his pencil from the paper.

40
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:47,980
He was overjoyed.

41
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:54,720
He did not know it but his little puzzle had started a whole new branch of mathematics called "topology".
